===Etymology 2===
From {{term|used||lang=en}}, past of {{term|use#Verb|use|perform habitually}} + {{term|to#Particle|to|lang=en|infinitive marker}}

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb|head=[[used]] [[to#Particle|to]]}}

# [[formerly|Formerly]]; once, but no longer; thus forming the [[imperfect tense]]
#: ''I '''used to''' be undecided, but now I’m not so sure.''
#: ''I '''used to''' like that band, but I don't like them anymore.''
#: ''I '''used to''' know a guy from the UK who pronounced "mother" without the "r".''
#* '''1980''', {{w|ABBA}}, ''{{w|The Winner Takes It All}}''
#*: But tell me does she kiss like I '''used to''' kiss you?
#*: Does it feel the same, when she calls your name?

=====Usage notes=====
* The following negative constructions: <ul><li>used not to; usedn't to; usen't to</li> <li>did not use to; didn't use to</li> <li>did not used to; didn't used to</li></ul> and their corresponding interrogative constructions: <ul><li>used [subject] to {{...}}?</li> <li>did [subject] use to {{...}}?</li> <li>did [subject] used to {{...}}?</li></ul> are all well attested, with different constructions being preferred in different regions at different times.
* When it is not necessary to include the following verb, in some regions it is usual to use the verb {{term|do}} as a stand-in (“He works harder than he used to do.”), whereas in others it is usual to use no verb at all (“He works harder than he used to”).
* In these senses, the word ''used'' is given the pronunciation ''yoost'' rather than the usual ''yoozd'' (as in "the key was used to open the door").

